<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">African Party for the Independence of Guinea and Cape Verde</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as PAIGC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The African Party for the Independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a> (PAIGC) was founded in 1956 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amilcar-cabral">Amílcar Cabral</a> and others to free both Portuguese colonies. From 1963 it waged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war in Portuguese Guinea that won control of much of the countryside and declared independence in 1973. It governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a> and, until 1980, Cape Verde, where its branch then became a separate party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paigc.jpg" alt="African Party for the Independence of Guinea and Cape Verde" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_PAIGC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bissau">Bissau</a> on 19 September 1956 as the African Party of Independence. It grew from a movement founded in 1954 by Henri Labéry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amilcar-cabral">Amílcar Cabral</a>, and had six founding members: Cabral, his brother Luís, Aristides Pereira, Fernando Fortes, Júlio Almeida and Elisée Turpin. Rafael Paula Barbosa was its first president and Cabral its secretary-general. After Portuguese soldiers fired on protesting dockworkers at Pidjiguiti in 1959, killing 50, the leadership turned to armed struggle and moved its headquarters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conakry">Conakry</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The armed struggle began in March 1962 with an unsuccessful attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praia">Praia</a>, and on 23 January 1963 the party opened the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a> by attacking a garrison at Tite. Fighting was concentrated on the mainland, and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a> islands the party worked clandestinely. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> sent artillery experts, doctors and technicians after Cabral met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a> in 1966, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> supplied weapons. By 1967 the party controlled two thirds of the colony.</p><p>Cabral was assassinated on 20 January 1973 by the naval commander Inocêncio Kani, in a plot within the party; Kani and at least ten others were executed. Independence was declared on 24 September 1973 and recognised by the UN General Assembly by 93 votes to 7 in November, before Portugal recognised it. By the end of the eleven-year war some 1,875 Portuguese soldiers and about 6,000 of the party's troops had been killed.</p><p>After independence the party was the sole legal party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a>, with Luís Cabral as president of Guinea-Bissau. A coup led by João Bernardo Vieira in November 1980 ended the plan for union, and the Cape Verdean branch became a separate party. Multi-party politics began in 1991; the party won in 1994, lost power in 1999-2000 and returned to government after the elections of 2004.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> the party received support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>. It was also the first African party to establish a comprehensive cooperative programme with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “African Party for the Independence of Guinea and Cape Verde” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>PAIGC</b>, founded by Amílcar Cabral in 1956, took up arms in Portuguese Guinea, and after the April 1974 revolution in Portugal it became an active movement in Cape Verde.</blockquote><a href="/state/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>PAIGC</b>, founded in 1956 under Amílcar Cabral, launched the Guinea-Bissau War of Independence on 23 January 1963 against Portuguese Guinea.</blockquote><a href="/state/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 20th century Cape Verdeans joined Amilcar Cabral's <b>PAIGC</b>, which fought for the independence of Portuguese Guinea and the islands.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-cape-verde">Portuguese Cape Verde</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1963 the <b>PAIGC</b>, led by Amilcar Cabral until his assassination in 1973, fought a guerrilla war that liberated much of the countryside.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-guinea">Portuguese Guinea</a></li></ul></section>
